            <ArticleTitle>Life style intervention in moderately overweight individuals is associated with decreased levels of cathepsins L and S in plasma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>283-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Adipose tissue cells produce cathepsins L and S, which have proatherogenic effects. Obesity is strongly linked to atherogenesis, cardiovascular morbidity, and mortality.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The aim of the present study was to see if life style interventions/weight reduction could decrease cathepsin L and S levels in blood plasma.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">Study subjects (n=31) were recruited to a life style intervention program aiming at increased physical activity, more healthy eating habits, and weight reduction for most of the participants. Blood samples were collected at inclusion and after 4 and 8 weeks.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Cathepsin L was significantly reduced at 4 weeks (p&lt;0.0001) and 8 weeks (p=0.0004). A similar reduction was also seen for cathepsin S at 4 weeks (p=0.03) and 8 weeks (p=0.008). No significant change in fractalkine values was observed at 4 weeks (p=0.58), but a significant increase was apparent at 8 weeks (p=0.0002).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The intervention program resulted in significant reductions of cathepsin L and S levels in plasma after 4 and 8 weeks of intervention.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2014 by the Association of Clinical Scientists, Inc.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
